%% Socket ticket authentication and tenant-scoped room lifecycle
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant Client as Customer/admin client
    participant REST as Socket-auth controller
    participant Context as Tenant context
    participant Ticket as SocketAuthService
    participant Gateway as Socket.IO gateway
    participant Redis
    participant DB as Tenant PostgreSQL
    participant Room as Scoped room registry

    alt Authenticated tenant user
        Client->>REST: POST /socket-auth/ticket
        REST->>Context: Read resolved organizationId
        REST->>Ticket: Require AuthGuard + TenantMembershipGuard
        Ticket-->>Client: Short-lived signed chat_socket ticket
    else Guest storefront
        Client->>REST: POST /socket-auth/guest-ticket with guest ID
        REST->>Context: Read resolved organizationId
        REST->>Ticket: Validate guest identifier and tenant context
        Ticket-->>Client: Short-lived signed guest ticket
    end

    Client->>Gateway: Connect with ticket
    Gateway->>Ticket: Verify signature, purpose, expiry, organization
    alt Ticket valid
        Gateway->>Room: Derive org-scoped personal/role/chat rooms
        Gateway->>Redis: Store tenant-scoped presence and socket ownership
        Gateway-->>Client: Connected with principal metadata
        Client->>Gateway: Join conversation or task room
        Gateway->>DB: Verify tenant membership/participant access
        Gateway->>Room: Add only authorized scoped room
    else Ticket invalid or organization missing
        Gateway-->>Client: Reject connection
    end

    Gateway->>Gateway: Persist chat message through tenant service
    Gateway->>Redis: Fan out only within org-prefixed rooms
    Gateway-->>Client: Tenant-scoped event

    Client-->>Gateway: Disconnect
    Gateway->>Redis: Remove socket/presence/page-view state
    Gateway->>Room: Clean up rooms and safe online status

    Note over Gateway,Room: Identical room names across organizations cannot share a channel.
    Note over Gateway,DB: Socket ticket is an access assertion, not a database credential.
